when we first moved here people thought  we were crazy there's a reason why  there's not a lot of farms here in  Minnesota  it's not much soil the climate is harsh  we knew the land could produce we  figured we'd figure it out  this great big story was made possible  by horizon organic I'm Lisa  Boz this is my husband David Abbas and  we are off-grid organic farmers in  Finland Minnesota  Finland is a small community along the  North Shore Lake Superior we've been  farming this land for last thirty plus  years people have said you can't farm in  Finland this soil was both infertile and  challenging that one you can't get a  shovel into the ground because it's full  with rocks but it didn't matter  everything it just felt right the soil  industrial farming treats it as a medium  to grow crops you insert the nutrients  and the crop grows but organic farming  it focuses on making that soil its own  living organism you have healthy soil  you end up with healthy plants and so we  focused on making sure we were feeding  the soil not just the plants this is all  good soil start to get a little denser  here through time through compost to  cover crops and care we're able to see  results and now the fertility is so much  greater than it's ever been  [Music]  our growing season can be as short as 33  days in the spring we grow lots of  greens we have lettuce spinach kale  we have radishes and beets we need water  we need food we need energy we've tried  to figure out how do we do all of those  everything we run is from solar and wind  power a water is pumped from the earth  we use a compost toilet and of course  you know what home said would be  complete without a flock of free-range  chickens  most of what we grow is distributed to  customers through a CSA which is  community supported agriculture it's a  way for small farmers to get dependable  sales of their products we sell about 50  shares which can feed a couple hundred  people for many years we've been  involved in the social fabric in the  community so we installed solar at the  fire station and then the community  center so that was all part of kind of  mainstreaming renewable energy into our  community sharing what we've learned by  living this way will hopefully make the  world better  you
 

